<header><p class="enc-kicker">Work / monument · monument</p><h1 class="enc-title">Place des Vosges</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1605">1605</a>–<a href="/years/1612">1612</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/17th-century">17th century</a> · Also known as Place Royale</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Place des <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vosges">Vosges</a> is a square in the Marais district of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/paris">Paris</a>, built between 1605 and 1612 on the orders of Henry IV and originally called the Place Royale. Surrounded by uniform houses of brick and stone above arcades, it is the oldest planned square in Paris and a model for later royal squares in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/europe">Europe</a>. It took its present name in 1800.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/place-des-vosges.jpg" alt="Place des Vosges" width="320" height="213" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Des Racines et des Ailes · CC BY 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Place_des_Vosges_vue_a%C3%A9rienne.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
